74
00:04:56,940 --> 00:04:58,860
if the rocket will get us there, you know.

75
00:04:58,860 --> 00:05:00,760
You know, it will be a risk a little bit.

76
00:05:02,580 --> 00:05:07,570
And does education have to stay the same as it's been for 200 years?

77
00:05:10,440 --> 00:05:18,700
In my book I wrote, referred to a book called The Tragedy of Education written in 1913 by Edmund Holmes.

78
00:05:19,460 --> 00:05:23,260
For 30 years he was a minister of education in England.

79
00:05:24,230 --> 00:05:29,710
And they had a real traditional English program then, you know, by the numbers, you know, you do this, this, and this.

80
00:05:29,710 --> 00:05:31,190
And he was enforcing it.

81
00:05:31,230 --> 00:05:36,210
He was the guy that had to make sure that everybody was on the same page at the same time, you know, that kind of thing.

82
00:05:38,270 --> 00:05:43,850
He said, finally, he said, 1911, he said, I can't take this anymore.

83
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:49,940
He wrote a book, 1911, what should be and what could be.

84
00:05:49,940 --> 00:05:57,260
In 1913, what tragedy of education described the current traditional system we have now.

85
00:05:58,360 --> 00:06:09,990
And he said, I apologize to all the parents and all the kids and all of the people that I did wrong.

86
00:06:09,990 --> 00:06:22,400
I spent the rest of my life trying to make up for what I did because the school system in England in the last 30 years has been all wrong.

87
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:24,780
And I've created a tragedy of education.

88
00:06:25,890 --> 00:06:32,250
This is the guy that was into it, you see, until he realized that this wasn't working.

89
00:06:34,730 --> 00:06:41,570
And Edmund, Bertrand Russell, a famous philosopher and mathematician, wrote a book in 1926 on education.

90
00:06:41,570 --> 00:06:49,180
He said, most of us as parents would like to send our kids to school for learning and socialization and so forth.

91
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:53,000
But we would like to send them to the schools that we have.

92
00:06:55,220 --> 00:07:00,200
He's a brilliant philosopher, straight-A person, the top mathematician in the country almost at the time, you see.

93
00:07:01,270 --> 00:07:06,010
And Margaret Mead in your history sociology kind of thing, Margaret Mead said,

94
00:07:06,650 --> 00:07:10,910
my grandmother wanted me to have a good education, so she kept me out of school.

95
00:07:10,910 --> 00:07:16,980
Seven U.S. presidents did not graduate from high school.

96
00:07:17,910 --> 00:07:22,730
The Wright brothers did not graduate from high school, yet they revolutionized transportation.

97
00:07:22,930 --> 00:07:25,430
They changed the world, didn't have a high school diploma.

98
00:07:26,850 --> 00:07:34,740
So, you see, we need the Wright brothers pioneering spirit, so we need that effort to try.

99
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:41,440
We can't recreate Wilson now, it's too late, and we wouldn't do it exactly the same anyway.

100
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:44,280
We didn't have computers at Wilson, remember when I did this thing?

101
00:07:44,280 --> 00:07:49,740
We had no cell phones, we had no computers, we had no iPods, or whatever you call them.

102
00:07:50,080 --> 00:07:57,890
So, now I think what Wilson might be like, but as it turns out, we were the first school in the state to have a computer.

103
00:07:59,640 --> 00:08:11,580
It looked like a little freedom, what do you call those boxes?

104
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:14,730
A freedom, adding machine kind of thing.

105
00:08:14,730 --> 00:08:16,590
It was kind of a little square box.

106
